;; An async import with nowhere to put the ambient token.
;;
;; §7.2's second rule is that the emitter fills in the `CancellationToken`, so a
;; method with no token overload cannot have one filled in — and the import has
;; to say so with `#:uncancellable`. Not inferred, deliberately: "this cannot be
;; stopped" is a fact about the call that its reader needs and its writer knows,
;; and the alternative is discovering it as a `choose` that leaks an operation
;; nobody is listening to any more.
;;
;; `Task.WhenAll` is the example on purpose. §7.7 says not to import it at all —
;; it collects every result and cancels nothing, which is the opposite of what a
;; `choose` over several promises wants — and this is the first thing that
;; happens if you try.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: has no overload taking a System.Threading.CancellationToken
;; EXPECT-ERROR: #:uncancellable

(import (std prelude))

(import/extern
  (when-all (: System.Threading.Tasks.Task.WhenAll #:async)))

(defbjo (main args) 0)
